{
  "id": "mambo",
  "name": "Mambo",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Cuban big-band brass-and-saxophone urban-popular-dance music codified 1939-onward in Havana from son-cubano accelerated-and-orchestrated for big-band swing-orchestra adaptation. The mambo-typical ensemble: four-trumpets, five-saxophones (two altos, two tenors, plus baritone), piano, contrabass, congas, bongos, timbales, claves, maracas, and güiro. The mambo-section open-improvisation montuno-equivalent features brass-shouts (mambos), saxophone-and-piano riffs over ostinato-vamp, and percussion-and-vocal call-and-response. The 1947-onward Mexico-City-and-NYC-Palladium mambo-craze made it the international Cuban-dance-music ambassador; the 1950s saw mambo-fused-with-jazz (Afro-Cuban jazz, Latin-jazz), with cha-cha-chá and pachanga emerging as next-generation variants.",
